  All four are 320x320 WebP, 6.0–10.6 KB, well under the 500 KB ceiling.

BUILD
  index.html — enamel plates: declaration hero (h1 + four mono telemetry wells
    + vermilion stamp), three feature plates indexed with leg bands, three
    statements with the one ID portrait, three-tier engagement schedule with a
    vermilion arrears note, closing plate handing off to the demo.
  demo.html  — Loft AN-04 payroll run console. Twelve named birds, jurisdiction
    filter chips, live search, per-row classification select that reprices the
    employer-liability figure immediately, detail drawer with observation log
    and filing history (Esc/scrim/button close, focus restored), and a RUN
    SETTLEMENT rail that steps four clearing stages and stamps either CLEARED
    or CLEARED WITH n EXCEPTIONS depending on how many birds are left
    unclassified or disputed.

WARNINGS / NEAR-MISSES
  - First validation run failed: the demo built its roster and drawer portraits
    as innerHTML strings, so validate-entry's <img src> scan matched the literal
    "' + b.pic + '" and reported a missing image. Rewritten to build the img
    elements with createElement via a mkImg() helper. Anything generating <img>
    from JS in future entries should do the same.
  - Vermilion #D6452F fails 4.5:1 on the bottle-green ground at body sizes, so
    small vermilion text uses a lightened tint of the same hue (#F0705A, ~4.9:1)
    while #D6452F is kept for floods, rules and the stamp border. Same rationed
    colour, still one warm accent on the page.

VISUAL CHECK (step 9, ran — playwright already installed)
  2 captures (landing, demo), 3 corrective edits, 1 re-capture. All at the cap.
  - Landing capture came back with everything below the hero invisible. The
    per-section fade-up was gated on an IntersectionObserver that only fires
    for elements entering a scrolling viewport, so a full-page render (and any
    no-JS or crawler context) got a page of empty plates. Removed the
    scroll-reveal entirely and replaced it with one load animation on the
    declaration plate. Content is no longer hidden behind JS, and the page lost
    a generic per-section-fade tell in the process. Re-capture confirms all six
    plates render.
  - Demo: the leg-band number ran flush into the worker name
    ("Ferdinand Two-RingPG-04471") because .bandsm was an inline span, so its
    margin-top did nothing. Set display:block.
  Screenshots are in docs/design-catalogue/ (gitignored).

